openModal closes existing modal before opening a new one, invoking its onClose. When clicking "Sell all duplicates" from duplicates modal, confirmAction opens, closes the duplicates modal (no onClose), fine. When a reward modal has onClose queueLevels, and another modal opens on top, it would trigger level up; not an issue. `modalCloseHandler` for reward modal is used.

Potential bug: `closeModal` calls onClose, which may itself open a modal (level up). It sets modalEl=null after calling fn? It calls fn then sets modalEl null; `flushLevelUps` checks `.modal-backdrop` in DOM, which is already removed, so opens new modal and sets modalEl. Then the outer closeModal continues and sets `modalEl = null` — losing the reference to the newly opened modal! That's a bug: In closeModal:
```
if (modalEl) {
  modalEl.remove();
  if (modalCloseHandler) { const fn = modalCloseHandler; modalCloseHandler = null; fn(); }
  modalEl = null;
}
```
If fn opens a new modal, its `openModal` calls closeModal again (modalEl still points to old removed node, modalCloseHandler null, removes again), sets new modalEl, then returns; outer then sets modalEl = null. The new modal exists in DOM but global reference lost, so Escape/close via data-close-modal still works? `closeModal` with modalEl null won't remove it, so the close button won't work! This is significant. Did this occur with the level-up modal? We closed the "4 rewards claimed" modal, its onClose queued level ups and opened the Level 3 modal. But the level-up modal appeared and then we clicked `.claim-modal [data-close-modal]` and it closed. How, if modalEl was null? Let's trace: The reward modal's `onClose` is set via `openRewardModal(... onClose: () => queueLevels(res.levelUps))`. `closeModal` removes the reward modal, calls fn -> queueLevels -> flushLevelUps checks `document.querySelector('.modal-backdrop')`. At this moment, has the reward modal DOM been removed? Yes, `modalEl.remove()` executed. It then opens `openLevelUpModal` (openModal). openModal calls closeModal: modalEl still points to the removed reward modal; removes again (no effect), modalCloseHandler is already null (set before fn? The code: `const fn = modalCloseHandler; modalCloseHandler = null; fn();` yes null); then modalEl is still old; openModal creates wrap, sets modalEl = wrap. returns. Outer closeModal continues `modalEl = null`. So after this, the level-up modal is in DOM, but modalEl=null. Clicking the "Keep racing" button closes via `closeModal`? The button has data-close-modal, handler calls closeModal; with modalEl null, it does nothing! So the level-up modal should have remained. But we clicked `.claim-modal [data-close-modal]` and our check after .4s did not verify modal count. The `playwright-cli click` output had a snapshot, but we didn't check. Maybe it didn't close! We then went on to other actions? Actually after we clicked the level-up modal close, we immediately inspected console; not verifying. The modal might still be open. Let's check now.

{
  "backdrops": 1,
  "modal": "✕\nCOLLECTOR LEVEL UP\nLEVEL 3\nPaddock Pass unlocked. Keep building the vault.\n◈\n5"
}
### Ran Playwright code
```js
await page.evaluate('() => ({backdrops:document.querySelectorAll(".modal-backdrop").length, modal:document.querySelector(".modal-backdrop")?.innerText.slice(0,80)})');
```

Yes! Severe bug confirmed: level-up modal cannot be closed because modalEl is null. The close button handler `closeModal` did nothing. We need to fix `closeModal` so that it clears `modalEl` before invoking the callback, not after. That way, if the callback opens a new modal, global reference is preserved.

Fix:
```
export function closeModal() {
  if (!modalEl) return;
  const el = modalEl;
  const fn = modalCloseHandler;
  modalEl = null;
  modalCloseHandler = null;
  el.remove();
  if (fn) fn();
}
```
But openModal calls closeModal at the start. If an existing modal has onClose that opens another modal, that would be weird; but clearing first prevents clobbering. Good.

Also, when `openModal` calls closeModal, if the existing modal's onClose opens a new modal, then openModal's subsequent creation would close it? It doesn't call closeModal again after creating; it would append a second. But in our flow, closing via button calls closeModal directly, callback opens new modal; outer doesn't do more. No conflict. For opening a modal while another is open and its onClose opens another, rare. The safest is as above.

After fix, the current stuck level-up modal in browser can be closed once the new JS loads? No, the page's module state has modalEl null. Reloading resets. We'll reload after fix.

Let's patch closeModal.

