I see! The issue: `hydrate` reconstructs the ledger from items, but it adds empty arrays for ALL items (not just serial ones). Actually the code is:

```js
for (const item of s.items) {
  const key = `${item.cardId}|${item.finish || 'base'}`;
  if (!Array.isArray(s.issued[key])) s.issued[key] = [];
  if (item.serial != null && !s.issued[key].includes(item.serial)) s.issued[key].push(item.serial);
}
```

It creates an empty `[]` for every item's key, even if the item has no serial. That's why 14 extra keys with empty arrays. This is harmless functionally (a `[]` entry for base cards), but it bloats the save and breaks JSON-order equality. More importantly, these empty arrays don't cause cap issues. But it's sloppy. Let me fix by only adding keys when there's a serial:

```js
for (const item of s.items) {
  if (item.serial == null) continue;
  const key = `${item.cardId}|${item.finish || 'base'}`;
  if (!Array.isArray(s.issued[key])) s.issued[key] = [];
  if (!s.issued[key].includes(item.serial)) s.issued[key].push(item.serial);
}
```

Also note: the "BEFORE KEYS" list has 40 keys but earlier we said keys before = 16. That's because the script ran `openPack` 3 times but the output shown might be from a different run? Actually no - the ADDED/BEFORE output was appended and re-run, so random results differ. Anyway.

Wait, there's a more serious issue revealed: the ledger has keys like `auto-senna-signed|vault`, `auto-senna-gold|goldsig`, etc. — but those are from `supplyInfo`/`usedSerials` calls? No, we didn't call them. Actually in `openPack('vault-signed')`, `injectCollectibles` calls `supplyAvailable(c, c.finish)` for all autograph specs, which calls `supplyInfo` → `usedSerials` → creates empty arrays in the ledger for every capped card! So the ledger gets 106 empty arrays after any pack open. That's fine but bloats the save. And on hydrate, only items with serials are processed, so those empty arrays persist from the save (they were saved). Hmm, actually the saved state has them, so they're preserved.

Actually the real concern: `usedSerials` mutates state.issued by creating empty arrays as a side effect of `supplyAvailable`. That's not harmful but it means every read creates entries. Could be optimized to not mutate on read:

```js
function usedSerials(card, finish) {
  const key = ledgerKey(card, finish);
  return Array.isArray(state.issued[key]) ? state.issued[key] : [];
}
```

But then `issueSerial` needs to create the array before pushing. Let me restructure:

```js
